<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sejong the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as King Sejong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sejong the Great was the fourth king of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ruling from 1418 to 1450. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-worthies">Hall of Worthies</a>, a royal research institute, and under him Korea created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> alphabet, rain gauges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clocks</a> and new works on agriculture and medicine. His reign also fixed Korea's northern border on the Yalu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> rivers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sejong was born as Yi To in 1397 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a>, now Seoul, the third son of Grand Prince Chŏngan, the future King Taejong, and Princess Chŏngnyŏng. His grandfather Taejo was the founder of Joseon. By 1406 Taejong, wishing to abdicate in his lifetime, had designated his eldest son, Grand Prince Yangnyŏng, as heir. In mid-1418 Yangnyŏng was deposed and Yi To, then Prince Ch'ungnyŏng, was made crown prince. Taejong abdicated on the 10th day of the 8th month of 1418, and the 21-year-old Sejong took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>. Taejong remained king emeritus until 1422.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sejong's reign saw major developments in science, medicine, agriculture and the arts. In 1420 he re-established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-worthies">Hall of Worthies</a>, a government research body. After a solar eclipse in 1432 came two days later than predicted, he commissioned astronomical instruments, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> was made in 1433 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a> designed by Chang Yŏngsil in 1434. In 1441 a rain gauge, the ch'ŭgugi, was invented under the supervision of the crown prince. The farming treatise Nongsa chiksŏl, completed in 1429, was meant to suit Korean agriculture more directly than Chinese works. In 1434 the court cast more than 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> kabin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable types</a>, twice as efficient as the earlier kyŏngja types.</p><p>In 1419 Sejong launched an expedition against Tsushima, followed by decades of peace and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s northern borders to roughly their present extent through campaigns against the raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a>. He reformed taxation, allowing peasants in 1421 to report their own crop assessments, and tried with limited success to raise the status of marginalised groups. His government restricted Buddhist institutions and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, although Sejong was privately Buddhist. Hangul, the Korean alphabet, is associated with him; scholars debate how far he created it personally, though most think he was significantly involved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sejong suffered from recurring and worsening health problems, including back pain and poor eyesight. From 1445 the crown prince, the future Munjong, handled daily government. Sejong died in 1450 at the age of 52 and was buried at Yeongneung. Historians have often described his reign as a golden age, and his name appears more than 2000 times in the Veritable Records. Hangul Day, a national holiday on 9 October, marks the alphabet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sejong the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1419, early in <b>Sejong</b>'s reign, the retired king Taejong sent an expedition against Tsushima to suppress Japanese pirates, and campaigns against the Jurchen in the 1430s and 1440s established the four commanderies and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the Yalu and Tumen rivers, where…</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ulfilas most likely invented the Gothic alphabet for his Bible; Cyril and Methodius devised Glagolitic for their mission to Moravia in 862 or 863; Mesrop Mashtots created the Armenian alphabet of thirty-six letters in 405; King <b>Sejong</b> of Korea promulgated Hangul in 1446.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>…names are a king under whom a thing was made and an artisan whose method a scholar wrote down: Hangul was created in 1443 under <b>Sejong the Great</b> of Joseon, and the movable type that Bi Sheng made about 1040 is recorded in the writings of Shen Kuo.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Sejong</b> tried to settle them as ordinary farmers under the old name for commoners, baekjeong, putting them on the registers and giving them land, but neighbours and even officials went on shunning them, and the name itself became an insult.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sejong the Great</b> of Joseon published the Hunminjeongeum, introducing a new phonetic script now called Hangul.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tongin-dong, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Andong Byeolgung. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.97&amp;year=1431" aria-label="Tongin-dong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7722 812 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sejong the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8172 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.97&amp;year=1431">What was here in 1431? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
